  "id": "spouge",
  "name": "Spouge (Barbadian ska-calypso)",
  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "Barbadian popular dance music created by the singer Jackie Opel in the mid-1960s after he had recorded ska in Jamaica, fusing Jamaican ska with Trinidadian calypso and drawing on hymns, spirituals and sea shanties. The original band sound rested on cowbell, bass guitar and trap set with other electric and percussion instruments, later augmented by saxophone, trombone and trumpets (the saxophone card carries the horn section); two styles emerged, raw spouge (the Draytons Two style) and dragon spouge (the Cassius Clay style), and spouge bands stayed popular through the 1970s.",
  "recipe": "classic tropical 1964-1966 spouge, functional Song, tropical song branch. solo with instrumental accompaniment. cencerro, low mid rich spectrum oily pore damping rosewood single coil electric guitar, electric bass, drum kit, saxophone. live tracking. dynamic moving-coil, tube pre, colored vintage console, vari-mu, Pultec passive program EQ. tape 15ips.",
